\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Orthogonal sums have product volume}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{Let #{E=E_1\mathbin\perp E_2} be an orthogonal direct sum of finite-dimensional
real inner-product spaces. Use their [canonical Euclidean volumes](fdip-002M)
on the uncompleted Borel sigma-algebras. Addition #{a(x_1,x_2)=x_1+x_2}
is a measure-preserving isometric isomorphism:
##{a_*(\lambda_{E_1}\otimes\lambda_{E_2})=\lambda_E.}
All these measures are sigma-finite. Nonnegative Borel functions therefore
satisfy Tonelli's iterated-integral identities. For a real Borel function
with finite absolute integral, Fubini holds and its sections are integrable
almost everywhere.}
\proof{
\p{Concatenate orthonormal bases of #{E_1} and #{E_2}. The resulting
orthonormal chart of #{E} turns addition into concatenation of coordinate
tuples. The [finite product splitting](fdip-002J) identifies the measures,
and chart independence is already proved at \ref{fdip-002M}. Empty bases
give singleton factors of mass one, so the argument includes zero summands.}
\p{Each finite chart is exhausted by cubes #{[-N,N]^n} of finite measure;
in dimension zero the whole measure has mass one. Thus
\citet{Theorem 1.7.15, pp. 200-201, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}
applies to these uncompleted sigma-finite products. Applying Tonelli to
the absolute value shows that section integrals of the absolute value are
finite almost everywhere. Applying it to positive and negative parts then
gives signed Fubini. The null-section statement in
\citet{Corollary 1.7.17, pp. 201-202, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}
concerns product-null sets; it does not remove the absolute-integrability
hypothesis for signed integrals.}
}
\p{This follows from canonical Euclidean volume and finite product splitting.
Mathlib's \leanref{LinearIsometryEquiv.measurePreserving} supports the
isometry step for finite-dimensional real Borel inner-product spaces with
canonical volume; arbitrary perfect pairings do not supply these hypotheses.}
